INT. NIGHT BOAT, RIVER DANUBE - NIGHT

A narrow cabin. Brass lamp. Curtains drawn. A tray with two untouched cups of tea.

OLGA RADOMSKA-IKEJIANI (40s, black gloves, a fur collar) sits on the bunk. She stirs her tea slowly, never lifting it.

Across from her, DETECTIVE KASPAR NWANKWO-BRUUN (50s, trench, bruised knuckles) leans against the door.

He is calm. She is calm. The boat rocks.

KASPAR
You knew him.

OLGA
Everyone knew him.

KASPAR
Better than most.

OLGA
Worse.

KASPAR
He wrote to you every week.

OLGA
Letters are cheap.

She sips. Sets the cup down. He watches the cup, not her.

KASPAR
He's dead, Olga.

OLGA
So I hear.

KASPAR
Poisoned.

OLGA
How careless.

He moves. In a single step he is across the cabin. She rises just as quickly. They stand an inch apart.

KASPAR (CONT'D)
(quietly)
Give me the glove.

OLGA
Which?

KASPAR
The left.

She holds out her left hand. He takes it. Peels the glove, finger by finger.

On her wrist: a bruise shaped like a ring of fingertips.

KASPAR (CONT'D)
He fought.

OLGA
He was stronger than he looked.

KASPAR
Than you looked.

She yanks free. He catches her again. They grapple. The lamp swings. Tea spills across the floor. She wrenches a hairpin from her hair, jabs at his arm. He twists it away. Both crash onto the bunk, breathing hard.

Then the cabin door opens. A woman's voice, off-screen, calm and amused:

LAURA (O.S.)
Kaspar. Olga. You're both very loud.

They freeze. Kaspar, over Olga, hand at her throat. Olga, under him, the pin at his ribs.

OLGA
(whispering)
Who is that?

KASPAR
(whispering back)
My wife.

Olga laughs, a low rich laugh. He lets go. She sits up, smoothing her fur.

OLGA
Then this is a very different kind of poisoning.
